      competitiveness: [72, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_nadia_csm1980, src_nadia_olympedia_bio, src_nadia_olympedia_1980aa | July 1980 | on-the-spot newspaper report + independent Olympic reference] In the Moscow 1980 team and individual competition she fell on bars earlier (the CSM notes she was not in the bars final), lost the all-around gold to a disputed margin of 0.075 (Olympedia: level second with 79.075 against 79.150), then fell on vault in the apparatus finals, and still won the balance-beam final and tied for the floor gold (CSM; Olympedia lists beam gold and floor tied gold). Olympedia adds that she won the 1981 World Student Games before retiring. One concentrated, well-documented episode of returning to competition after setbacks within days, not a decade-long pattern, so only a moderate score; her own memoir remarks on hard work are not used here.
      persistence: [66, 0.55, "d", "A"],
      // [src_nadia_gymhist1973, src_nadia_cbs2004, src_nadia_olympedia_bio | 1973-2004 | contemporaneous state-press observation + her own remarks; not independently corroborated] A journalist who attended several of her 1973 training sessions wrote that behind her performances was 'an enormous amount of work' and that she practised 'hours, days, weeks, months' for a single element, dividing her day between school and the training room; her coach added that her training load was carefully monitored. In 2004 she said 'Working hard is a part of life and I don't complain about working hard.' The training regime was set by coaches and the national programme (see the RFE/RL context source, not scored), so how much is her own discipline versus imposed structure cannot be separated; consistent with discipline but kept below documented-pattern level.
      discipline: [62, 0.45, "s", "A"],
      // [src_nadia_cbs2004, src_nadia_gymhist1973, src_nadia_ap2020 | 1973-2020 | her own statements + contemporaneous judge's remark + AP interview] Self-report: 'I love to learn skills and I love to accomplish ... Take little steps', and that she does not want people to train just to reach the Olympics (2004). In 1973 a judge told a journalist that her exercises contained 'only elements of the highest degree of difficulty'; in 1976 she said her goal was 'not to make a major mistake and hit the ground' rather than chasing a score (AP 2020). The memoir also reports that a skill named after her is rated the top difficulty tier, which is her own claim and not independently checked here. Mostly self-reported plus one judge's remark; moderate score and confidence.
