      // [src_bolt_bbc_daegu2011, src_bolt_wa_moscow2013, src_bolt_guardian_kessel2012, src_bolt_abc_beijing2015 | 2011-2015 | race reports + governing-body report | independent of Bolt] After the Daegu 2011 false-start disqualification in the 100 m, which he first met with 'I have nothing to say right now. I need some time' (BBC), he went on to win the 200 m at those championships (World Athletics 2013 summary). In 2012 he won the Olympic 100 m after a run of doubts and a defeat by his training partner Yohan Blake weeks earlier (Guardian), and in 2015 he recovered from a stumble in the semi-final to win the final by 0.01 s (ESPN/ABC). Three separate, dated recoveries from setbacks in independent reports; scored a clear lean rather than higher because the sources describe results, not the effort behind them.
      persistence: [66, 0.58, "d", "A"],
      // [src_bolt_nbc_first100, src_bolt_abc_au2025, src_bolt_guardian_hattenstone2010 | 2007-2008 | press articles quoting coach Mills (via the Gleaner) and Bolt's own account | two accounts of one episode] In 2007 his coach Glen Mills preferred that he move up to the 400 m; Bolt proposed a deal under which a fast 100 m would let him double 100 m and 200 m in 2008. He broke Don Quarrie's national 200 m record, Mills is quoted as saying 'He just said, When is the 100m?', and three weeks later he ran his first professional 100 m in Crete in 10.03. He also pleaded with Mills to run the 100 m at Beijing (Guardian 2010). One episode told twice (the two accounts differ on the exact terms), so a modest score.
